WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.558
Book Three, Proposition Ten.

2
00:00:02.558 --> 00:00:06.759
A circle does not cut a circle at more points than two.

3
00:00:06.759 --> 00:00:14.930
For, if possible, let the circle A B C cut the circle D E F at more points than two, namely B, G, F, and H.

4
00:00:15.350 --> 00:00:23.460
Join the common chords B H and B G by Postulate One, and bisect them at the points K and L by One Ten.

5
00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:33.160
From K and L, draw K C and L M at right angles to B H and B G by One Eleven, and carry them through to the points A and E.

6
00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:43.723
Because in the circle A B C the segment A C bisects the chord B H at right angles, the center of A B C lies on A C by the Porism to Three One.

7
00:00:43.723 --> 00:00:52.509
And because in the same circle the segment N O bisects the chord B G at right angles, the center of A B C also lies on N O.

8
00:00:52.509 --> 00:00:59.937
Since the segments A C and N O meet at no point except P, the point P is the center of the circle A B C.

9
00:00:59.937 --> 00:01:07.794
By the exact same reasoning on the common chords B H and B G, P is also the center of the second circle D E F.

10
00:01:07.794 --> 00:01:16.080
Thus the two circles A B C and D E F which cut one another have the same center P—which is impossible by Three Five.

11
00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:20.794
Therefore, a circle does not cut a circle at more points than two.

12
00:01:20.794 --> 00:01:22.580
As was required to prove.

13
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:37.013
As Sir Thomas Heath observes, Euclid's proof of Three Ten never assumes that the circles cross one another—it proves universally that two distinct circles cannot share more than two common points, whether they intersect or touch.

14
00:01:37.013 --> 00:01:53.840
In the GEOMETOR G-Index, node G one-hundred-eight combines the chord-bisector porism of Three One with the non-concentricity theorem of Three Five, serving as a foundational prerequisite for thirty-three later propositions across Books Three, Four, Six, Twelve, and Thirteen.

15
